    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential tremor (G25.0)</content>. Partial response to propranolol 60 mg: TETRAS performance 17 (from 22), activities of daily living 14 (from 18), writing item 2. Pulse 64 and blood pressure 122/76 mmHg without symptoms leave room to increase. [S]<list><item>Increase propranolol extended-release to 80 mg each morning.</item><item>Review in four weeks; primidone remains the alternative if a higher dose is not tolerated.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hazardous drinking (Z72.89)</content>. About 10 drinks a week with no day above three, within the agreed limits. [S]<list><item>Continue; GGT and liver tests at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Tremor rating: TETRAS performance and activities of daily living subscales</td><td>At each visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Performance 17 (from 22) and activities of daily living 14 (from 18); writing item 2</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Archimedes spiral and handwriting sample (the same sentence each time, dated and kept in the chart)</td><td>At each visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right spiral narrower with a smaller tremor axis; handwriting smaller and more even, still uneven at the signature</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Beta-blocker safety: resting pulse, seated and standing blood pressure, wheeze, fatigue, cold hands, low mood, exercise tolerance</td><td>At each visit after starting or changing the dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pulse 64; blood pressure 122/76 mmHg with no postural drop; no wheeze, fatigue, cold hands, or low mood</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Drugs and substances that worsen tremor</td><td>At each medication change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No decongestants taken during the cold; caffeine two cups; alcohol within limits</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Drinking review after the brief intervention (drinks a week, heaviest day, use of alcohol to steady the hand)</td><td>At each visit after a brief intervention</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 10 drinks a week, no day above three</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>GGT and liver tests after reducing alcohol</td><td>Eight to twelve weeks after the brief intervention</td><td>Due</td><td>Due at the next visit, about ten weeks after the brief intervention</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy evaluation for writing</td><td>Within two to four weeks of referral</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Missed: the occupational therapy evaluation requested at diagnosis four weeks ago has not taken place because of a waiting list. No harm: his writing is improving on propranolol and he is managing with a heavier pen. Corrective action: the clinic secured an appointment next week with another therapist and gave him a sheet on pen grips and forearm support in the meantime</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Primidone (sedation, unsteadiness, nausea)</td><td>Only if used</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not started; propranolol is effective and tolerated</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI or dopamine transporter imaging</td><td>Only for atypical features (rest tremor, parkinsonism, asymmetry with other signs, sudden onset, ataxia)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No atypical features; the diagnosis is clinical</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Deep brain stimulation or focused ultrasound thalamotomy</td><td>Only for disabling tremor despite adequate trials of two first-line drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Tremor responding to the first drug</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Classify the tremor and exclude treatable causes by the second visit. Outcome by 1616-01-26: Essential tremor diagnosed; TSH, glucose, calcium, and kidney tests normal; no parkinsonism [S]</item><item>Legible handwriting for a full page and a steady signature. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item><item>No more than 14 standard drinks a week and no more than 4 on any day. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item><item>Resting pulse above 55 without dizziness, wheeze, or fatigue on propranolol. In progress; target 1616-04-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline update: treatment of essential tremor: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2011; reaffirmed 2025) (2011); Consensus Statement on the classification of tremors, from the task force on tremor of the International Parkinson and Movement Disorder Society (2018); MDS evidence-based review of treatments for essential tremor (2019); Unhealthy Alcohol Use in Adolescents and Adults: Screening and Behavioral Counseling Interventions (2018); Helping Patients Who Drink Too Much: A Clinician&apos;s Guide (2005)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week review on propranolol 80 mg with GGT and liver tests, a tremor rating, and pulse and blood pressure. Next scheduled visit 1616-03-23: eight-week review: tremor and writing, propranolol at 80 mg, liver tests, and drinking. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
